## Goal and hypothesis
Does serving four requests at once raise the total throughput without slowing each request much? Expected: about 3 times the total tokens per second, and each request losing less than 25% of its speed.

## Setup
Table: Setup
| Item | Value |
| --- | --- |
| Model | 8B parameters, 4-bit weights |
| Prompt | 512 tokens, the same for every request |
| Output | 256 tokens, temperature 0 |
| Runtime | one local server, default settings |

## Method
1. Load the model once and send one warm-up request.
2. Send 1, 2, 4 and 8 requests at the same moment.
3. Repeat each size five times and keep the median.

## Results
Table: Median of five runs
| Requests at once | Total tokens per second | Per request |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 1 | 38 | 38 |
| 2 | 66 | 33 |
| 4 | 104 | 26 |
| 8 | 141 | 18 |

```chart kind=bar title="Total throughput" y="tokens per second"
categories: 1, 2, 4, 8
series Total: 38, 66, 104, 141
```

## Observations
- Four requests give 2.7 times the throughput of one, close to the 3 times expected.
- Each request slows by 32% at four requests, so the second half of the hypothesis fails. At two requests the loss is 13% and it holds.
- Going from four to eight adds only 36% more throughput.
> [!key] Takeaway
> Serve two requests at once when people are waiting, and four when only the total matters.

## Next experiments
- [ ] Repeat with a 4,000-token prompt, where the KV cache starts to matter
- [ ] Repeat with 8-bit weights
